Employee Management System
Object-Oriented Design in C#
# Project Overview

This project is a structured implementation of an Employee Management System built in C# to demonstrate strong foundational software engineering principles.

Rather than focusing only on functionality, this project emphasizes:

Clean object-oriented architecture

Separation of concerns

Extensibility without modifying existing logic

Proper use of runtime polymorphism

The system models multiple employee types and dynamically calculates bonuses using inheritance and method overriding.

# Architecture & Design

The system is built around a base Employee class that defines shared properties and behavior, including:

Name

Id

CalculateBonus() (virtual method)

ToString() (formatted output)

Specialized employee types extend this base class:

HourlyEmployee

SalaryEmployee

Each derived class overrides CalculateBonus() with its own implementation, allowing the system to process employees generically while executing type-specific behavior at runtime.

This eliminates conditional logic (if/switch) and casting, demonstrating proper polymorphic design.

# Key Engineering Decisions
1️⃣ Business Logic Inside Domain Classes

Bonus calculations are encapsulated within each employee type.
The Main program orchestrates execution but does not perform calculations.

This enforces single responsibility and improves maintainability.

2️⃣ Constructor Chaining

Derived classes pass shared properties to the base class constructor, ensuring consistent and reliable object initialization.

3️⃣ Polymorphic Processing

Employees are stored in a single collection:

List<Employee> employees = new List<Employee>();

The system processes all employee types uniformly:

foreach (Employee emp in employees)
{
    Console.WriteLine(emp.CalculateBonus());
}

The correct overridden method executes at runtime without type checking.

This demonstrates clean extensibility and adherence to the Open/Closed Principle.

